# Your own projects. These hold real photos and marks, and the app creates the
# folder on first run, so it is never something to track.
projects/

# Briefs written to hand one working session over to the next. They describe a
# moment -- this branch, these marks, this number to beat -- so they are stale
# within days and read as instructions to somebody who is no longer there. The
# durable version of anything in them belongs in a docstring next to the code it
# explains, or in a commit message. A pattern rather than two names because the
# habit keeps producing them.
*-HANDOFF.md

# Generated demo scenes. Both are rebuilt in one command, so the photos do not
# belong in history:
#   .venv\Scripts\python.exe demo\make_demo.py
#   .venv\Scripts\python.exe demo\make_rig_demo.py
demo/photos/
demo/project.multispy
demo_rig/
demo_practice/
demo_rig_practice/
demo_blank/

# Export output, written next to whichever project produced it
export/
**/export/

# Virtual environment (~220 MB)
.venv/
venv/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Editors and OS
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Static build output (rebuilt by tools/build_static.py)
dist/
tests/_scratch_sensorcheck/

# Per-machine launch configuration for the Browser pane. Deliberately not
# tracked: every entry names a project under projects/, which is itself
# gitignored because it holds the user's own photos and marks. A committed copy
# would point a fresh clone at folders it does not have, which is the same class
# of fault tests.yml exists to catch.
.claude/

# Diagnostic plots written next to the tool that produced them
# (tools/crossval.py --cull-sweep, and the residual field). Output, not source:
# regenerate rather than read an old one, since neither records which solve it
# came from.
/cull-sweep.png
/residual-field.png

# Project save archives dropped in the working folder. Tens of megabytes each
# and permanent once committed, so they are excluded by name rather than left
# to be noticed in `git status`.
*Save.zip
